      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
        "buildHTML": "Generate both the HTML and CSS at the same time.",
        "buildJavaScript": "The student has planned a dynamic feature using these four parts:\n- Event: what the user does\n- State: what data is tracked\n- Logic: what decides the change\n- Update: what changes on the page\n\nAsk the student to share their plan for each of these four parts. Then, using their plan, generate the JavaScript in `script.js` that adds this feature to their existing HTML and CSS. \n\nKeep the code beginner-friendly:\n- Use clear variable names that match the student's plan\n- Use a single event listener and event handler\n- Include a variable for state, a condition if the logic needs one, and a function if it helps organize the update\n- Do not add features the student did not ask for\n\nAfter generating the code, briefly explain how the event, state, logic, and update appear in the code."
      }
    },
    "long_instructions": "## Do This:\r\n\r\n1. **Generate the UI:** Plan in `planning.md`, then prompt AI to create the content and styling.\r\n\r\n2. **Build the Feature:** Plan in `planning.md`, then prompt AI to add your dynamic feature.\r\n\r\n3. **Label:** In `script.js`, add comments to label the element selector, event listener, and event handler. Inside the handler, label any variable, condition, or function.\r\n\r\n4. **Evaluate:** Check your feature against POUR:\r\n   - Perceivable — Can users notice what changed?\r\n   - Operable — Can users trigger it easily?\r\n   - Understandable — Do users know what happened?\r\n   - Robust — Does it work reliably?\r\n\r\n5. **Improve:** Identify the weakest part and prompt AI to help you fix it.\r\n",
    "teacher_markdown": "### **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n **Students may jump into prompting before planning the feature**\r\n\r\nSome students may ask AI to “make an app” before deciding what the app does or how the user interacts with it. Encourage them to complete the planning doc first by asking: **\"What is the user going to do, and what should change on the page?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant planning sections:\r\n```markdown\r\nEvent (what the user does):\r\nState (what data is tracked):\r\nLogic (what decides the change):\r\nUpdate (what changes on the page):\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may create a UI without a clear dynamic feature**\r\n\r\nSome students may generate a page that looks complete but does not have meaningful interaction. Help them identify the behavior by asking: **\"What happens after the user clicks, types, selects, or moves something?\"** Remind students that the feature should include an event listener and an event handler.\r\n\r\nRelevant code labels students might add:\r\n```javascript\r\n// Element selector\r\n// Event listener\r\n// Event handler\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may label the code without understanding it**\r\n\r\nStudents may add comments randomly just to satisfy the requirement. Encourage them to explain each label by asking: **\"Which line finds the element? Which line waits for the user action? Which lines run after the action happens?\"**\r\n\r\nExample labels:\r\n```javascript\r\n// Element selector\r\nconst button = document.getElementById(\"myButton\");\r\n\r\n// Event listener\r\nbutton.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n  // Event handler\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may evaluate only whether the feature works, not whether users can use it well**\r\n\r\nSome students may stop once the feature technically works. Use POUR to push them toward accessibility and usability by asking: **\"Can users notice the change, trigger it easily, understand what happened, and rely on it working every time?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant checklist:\r\n```markdown\r\nPerceivable — Can users notice what changed?\r\nOperable — Can users trigger it easily?\r\nUnderstandable — Do users know what happened?\r\nRobust — Does it work reliably?\r\n```"
  },
